  "description": "Detects and repairs mojibake: text garbled by an encoding mistake, such as \"cafe\" arriving as \"cafA(c)\" or an apostrophe turning into a three-character sequence. A focused .NET port of the core of Python's ftfy (fixes text for you) fix_encoding algorithm, ported verbatim from ftfy 6.3.1 including its badness heuristic, its sloppy Windows-1252/1251/1250/1253/1254/1257 codecs, restore_byte_a0, replace_lossy_sequences, decode_inconsistent_utf8, the utf-8-variants (CESU-8) decoder, and the C1 control fixer. The cardinal rule is that repair never makes text worse: if no round-trip lowers the text's badness, the input is returned unchanged, so it is safe to run over an entire dataset including already-clean rows. .NET has had no way to repair an already-decoded broken string; UTF.Unknown only detects the charset of raw bytes. Includes fix-and-explain auditing, a LooksLikeMojibake detector, and an exposed badness score. Verified for exact parity against a real ftfy 6.3.1 run over a large corpus. Zero external dependencies.",
